A||||7||Uaana||Posi?||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||12-20-2000||04:03 AM||||Ok just trying to figure this out. Do all Fiero's have Posi rear ends?<BR>Every time I pop the gas in snow I get a two wheel spin. I remember my old Rwd's and Fwd's only spinning one unless I did the gas pop manuver to lock up the limited slip.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-20-2000||11:13 AM||||Well, technically FWD trannies are not "posi" (the Fiero tranny is a FWD design). However, they are completely different than the old style RWD trannies and rear ends, so yes both tires will usually spin.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||12-20-2000||01:38 PM||||Batboy is right.  There are no clutches in the differentials in our Fieros.  However due to the arrangment of the gear set they usually apply power to both wheels and lay down two black stripes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-21-2000||10:11 AM||||Are 88's the same? I know whenever I get my tires spining in my coupe (not often lol) its allways both rear.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||12-21-2000||11:10 PM||||<BR>    I Have owned two Fiero's in my life and yes In a straight line Both wheels will spin (manuals tend to jump out of line a bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) BUT Auto-X your car once or drive aggressively (sp) on a realy twisty road and will you light up the inside wheel in any gear.<P>..................<BR>Force fed 84 SE (parts)<BR>Bottle fed SE (gave it away)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||12-22-2000||07:53 AM||||All years are the same as far as not having limited slip or posi.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||12-25-2000||05:33 AM||||yep.  I found out how it worked when I left 2 black marks on my driveway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seems that if you start putting out a lot more power than stock, you'll only light up the drivers side tire... I have a video of Archie's blue chop top doing a burnout and only the driver's side tire leaves a streak...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||12-25-2000||07:50 AM||||Arch correct me if I'm wrong but I'm pretty sure that because of the V8's slightly off center position you have more weight on the pass. side tire hence easier to break the drivers side loose. On my 3800 the side to side weight dist. is just about 50/50 and both tires break loose. So I don't think it's a power thing.||||reg||1
